Driving simulators have become a central development tool during the recent decades. Advantages such as reproducible and fully parameterised scenarios, safe testing of critical driving situations, flexibility for complex variant comparisons, and the possibility to experience new car functions a long time before they are available in real prototypes have meant that these development tools are almost without alternative in concept development phases. But there are also some challenges when using driving simulators, such as the big variety of use cases with different requirements which prevents the use of one simulator for all investigations (Fischer et al. 2015; Hartfiel et al. 2018). Classical driving simulators are almost always equipped with displays or projection systems for visualisation and a mock-up as representation of the cockpit. Especially for the mockup, engineers need to decide whether they want a realistic appearance to improve the immersion of the simulation or a generic version (e.g. consisting of item profile) to have more flexibility for positioning control elements. Both variants have drawbacks for the evaluation of new interior concepts. On the one hand, there is a realistic appearance, but there

