are limited options for replacing components. On the other hand, a generic mock-up is too abstract to create a feeling of sitting in a car. To support the development of new interiors, both advantages should be combined in a single tool. Virtual reality (VR) techniques offer some advantages. When using 3D-projection systems such as cave automatic virtual environments (CAVE) or head-mounted displays (HMD) for visualisation, all components can be virtual. Therefore, a simulation can be simultaneously highly immersive and flexible. In combination with some haptic dummies (e.g. for the steering wheel and displays), the driver–car interaction also becomes realistic. In some high-end driving simulators, CAVEs have been in use for years, but drawbacks like the expensive costs and the large amount of space required prevent a more frequent use also in smaller simulators. By contrast, HMDs with their restrictions regarding resolution, field of view (FOV) and the accommodation–vergence conflict were rather the exception in driving simulation context (Blissing and Bruzelius 2018). However, since new generations of HMDs were released in 2015–2016 (Oculus Rift, HTC Vive, FOVE), this visualisation system is becoming an alternative for driving simulators. But because of the still existing drawbacks (e.g. the additional weight on the head) and other properties such as the complete decoupling from reality, influences with regard to the validity of driver behaviour must be taken into account.
2 HMDs in driving simulators
Blissing and Bruzelius (2018) investigated the suitability of HMDs for studies in the driving simulation context and reviewed 203 papers from the two largest conferences (Driving Simulation Conference, Road Safety and Simulation Conference). They summarised that only 4 studies

were conducted using a HMD-based simulator but also that 40% of all analysed studies could have been performed using an HMD. In addition, 47 papers (published between 1997 and 2019) in which HMDs were used as visualisation systems in a driving simulation context were investigated in the preparation of this publication. 24 of these papers were published between 1997 and 2015 and 23 between 2016 and 2019. 12 papers focused only theoretical/technical overviews or explanations without practical evaluations. In 35 papers, results from studies are reported. Four of them report research results for ergonomic investigations without driving. In 24 studies, very simple desktop simulators with game components or static simulators with mock-ups were used (Fig. 1).
7 studies were carried out in more complex simulators with motion bases. In two of these papers (Hirata et al. (2007), Ropelato et al. (2017), very small 2 and 6 DOF systems were used (e.g. with 65 mm range of motion). In two other papers, participants drove in systems with bigger motion bases, but the HMD visualisation worked only without head tracking (Erler and Rinderknecht 2017; Grabe et al. 2010). In the remaining three papers, driving simulators with bigger motion bases and head tracking were used (Hartfiel and Stark 2019; Tiemann 2019; Schill et al. 1997).
After analysing the studies, it could be summarised that 75% of the used simulators were static low or medium fidelity systems. Particularly for low budget simulators, the use of an HMD for visualisation is very attractive. Visual immersion levels can be achieved that could previously only be achieved with much more expensive systems like CAVEs or 3D projections. This has resulted in a much lower threshold for simulations of driving situations with high immersive visualisations. But it also increases the risk that simple and very fast built systems are used for investigations without previous validations.

