3 Transferability of results from the simulated to the real world

4 Validity of primary driving tasks in HMD‑based driving simulators

Simulators are normally used to investigate issues that would be too expensive, dangerous or take too long under real conditions. Therefore, the central goal for all simulators is the transferability of results to a real system. A quality criterion to describe the possibilities to transfer results from a simulated condition to a real one is the validity. Validity can be distinguished into physical and behavioural validity (Blaauw 1982; Mudd 1968). In the context of driving simulators, physical validity describes the physical correspondence between a simulated and a real driving environment. Physical validity can be improved, using a real car cockpit as mock-up or by visualising it in a virtual scene. It can also be improved if high-quality vehicle dynamic models are used in combination with motion bases.
Behavioural validity describes the correspondence between a simulated and a real driving scenario in the way that a participant behaves. Rolfe et al. (1970) describe it as: “The value of a simulator depends on its ability to elicit from the operator the same sort of response that he would make in the real situation”. Other researchers such as Blana (1996) and Zöller et al. (2013) support this position and add that behavioural validity is in general more important than physical validity. Higher physical validity would be not enough, if the behaviour of the participant would be different to real conditions. On the other hand, behavioural validity can be reached with a simulator that does not have highest physical validity. In addition, behavioural validity can be differentiated into absolute and relative validity. Hirata et al. (2007) described these two criteria in this way: “Absolute validity is achieved if the numerical values between two systems are the same. […] Relative validity is achieved if the differences found between experimental conditions are in the same direction, and have a similar or identical magnitude both real world and the simulator”.
Törnros (1998) reports that relative validity is required for a driving simulator. However, absolute validity is not essential. He argues that researchers often investigate effects of independent variables or differences between sample groups and not specific numerical values. But it isn’t always easy to define borders from where control groups are similar and relative validity can be assumed. Therefore, the initial goal for all simulators stays to reach absolute validity.
Blaauw (1982) distinguishes between different methods to analyse behavioural validity. Besides the classical comparison between a simulated and a real condition, validity could be also investigated with comparing physiological parameters. Such comparisons are also common in the driving simulation context.

This paper focuses on investigating the gaze behaviour in HMD-based driving simulators with the aim of evaluating the validity of primary driving tasks. Additionally, other parameters like physiological variables, speed-, accelerationor steering- behaviour are also important for validations and have been taken in account.
4.1 Physiological parameters
Simulator sickness, immersion and presence are important indicators that realistic driver behaviour could be reached. Analysis of studies such as Aykent et al. (2014, 2015), Cao et al. (2019), Reich and Stark (2016), Ruspa et al. (2000) and Walch et al. (2017) comparing different visualisation systems revealed that HMDs led to higher levels of immersion and presence feeling but also higher simulator sickness scores than screens or 2D projection systems. The authors conclude that HMDs led generally to more simulator sickness scores compared to other visualisation systems without enough investigations about possible reasons. However, studies such as Weidner et al. (2017), Riecke et al. (2005) and Colombet et al. (2016) comparing HMDs with 3D-projection systems or CAVEs show instead comparable values for the parameters. Additionally, Hartfiel and Stark (2019) reveal that the increase of other cues (e.g. motion cues) led to a more homogeneous simulation and also lower simulator sickness scores. The same correlation can be seen in many studies for other visualisation systems. However, it could be argued that for HMDs just the risk for getting high simulator sickness scores is higher when the remaining simulator architecture is not appropriate for such high visual induced immersions. Therefore, the risk of getting invalid results from studies is also high, and comparisons to test runs where real cars were used are important. When looking through the studies analysed for this publication, it is noteworthy that only 2 of the 47 studies have done comparisons to real test runs.
4.2 Gaze behaviour
Since only one publication (Burns and Saluäär 1999) was found that contains validations of gaze behaviour parameters in an HMD-based driving simulator, studies such as Reich (2017) and Victor et al. (2005) on driving simulators with other visualisation systems were also analysed. Additionally, investigations of gaze behaviour in VR in other research contexts are taken into account Berton et al. (2019) and Brument et al. (2019).

