Burns and Saluäär (1999) conducted a validation study in which they compare and analyse the visual behaviour of 11 participants in a static driving simulator with HMD visualisation and a real car. A t-junction is chosen as scenario. The results show that there is no difference in the number of glances, but drivers make significant longer fixations in VR than they did in the real test runs. Burns and Saluäär (1999) justify these findings with a need for longer glance times in VR to figure out whether cars are crossing the junction. Together with findings for other parameters, they summarise that driving in this HMD-based simulator is significantly different compared to driving in reality. One of the main reasons which they use for argumentation of the different gaze behaviour is the high proportion of simulator sickness (5 of 11 participants) during the simulator test runs. Although the results are already 21 years old, they are also relevant for these studies.
In the further explanations, studies that focus on gaze behaviour, but were carried out in driving simulators with other visualisation systems (e.g. screens or projection systems) were analysed.
In her doctoral thesis, Reich (2017) examined influences of visual and aurally parameters with regard to a valid driver behaviour. Comparisons of simulator variants with different immersion levels and test runs in a real car were performed. The task for the participants consisted of typing in addresses in a navigation system and perform Lane change tasks (LCT). The results show that participants looked less often to the navigation system in the high immersive variant. Reich (2017) explains these results with differences in the participants’ sense of risk in the different test cases. She argues mainly that the participants in the low immersive simulator environment looked more often than in the real car longer than 1.6 s and 2 s to the navigation system. In the end, Reich concluded that the high immersive simulator variant is closer to real driving than the others and some parameters even show absolute validity.
Victor et al. (2005) carried out a study where they compared the performance of secondary tasks in different scenarios (motorway, rural roads) and driving simulators (two static simulators, one with a curved screen (135° FOV) the other one with a 2D projection system (150° FOV)). They also divide the tasks into different difficulty classes (Level 1–3). Although the focus of the study was not on the comparison between the real and the simulator environments, some findings were reported. The results show that the mean glance duration and the total glance duration are significant longer in the simulator than in the real world. They also reported significant differences in single difficulty levels for the parameters glance frequency and the percentage that participants looked to the road centre. These findings correlate with Reich’s thesis that the sense of risk is lower in the low

fidelity simulators and therefore participants looked longer off the road (e.g. to navigation systems).
Because only one study Burns and Saluäär (1999) was found, where the gaze behaviour in a HMD-based driving simulator was investigated, additional studies about gaze behaviour in HMDs in other contexts are analysed.
Berton et al. (2019) investigated the gaze behaviour during collision avoidance between people in different virtual setups compared to the real one. The virtual environments were a Screen, HMD and a CAVE. Apart from the number of glances in the CAVE, the results show similar values for the other test environments. The authors argued the lower values in the CAVE with the lighting conditions which may have affected the eye tracking system. In general, they summarised that the values measured in the HMD come closest to the real ones.
Brument et al. (2019) carried out a study where participants should perform an eight-shaped trajectory using different strategies (walking, steering, beaming) in an HMD-based environment. Even if the results show similarities between the VR and real environments, they reported differences. They reported e.g. that the gaze anticipates more the change in heading in VR than in the real environment. The authors explained these results with three different points. First, the small FOV of the HMD could have an impact on the gaze anticipation and also the head movements. The second influence could be that the participants don’t see their own body in VR and therefore they did more gaze anticipation. Third, the weight of the HMD could influence head movements and also gaze trajectories.
The previous analysis about the state of the art for eye tracking in the driving simulation context reveal on the one hand that there are a very limited number of investigations about the gaze behaviour in HMD-based driving simulators. Only one paper Burns and Saluäär (1999) was found, which was published over 20 years ago, where the technical possibilities were very limited. Therefore, findings may no longer stay true. On the other hand, studies in driving simulators with other visualisation systems show that valid gaze behaviour is in principle possible and could correlate with the level of presence of the whole simulation. In conclusion, the findings show that additional researches into the gaze behaviour of participants in relation to different driving simulator types are necessary.
4.3 Driving dynamics parameters
In order to examine the validity of primary driving tasks in HMD-based driving simulators, further parameters have to be considered. As mentioned in Sect. 4.1 the number of studies with comparisons between such simulators and real test runs is currently very limited (2 of 47 papers).

