Burns and Saluäär (1999) not only reported results of the gaze behaviour, they also evaluated the validity of the speed behaviour in their validation study. Burns and Saluäär (1999) results show that in the t-junction scenario with the static HMD-based simulator, participants drove significantly slower compared to the real-world condition. They come to the conclusion that their simulator fidelity is not enough to create a valid driver behaviour.
Hirata et al. (2007) developed a compact HMD-based driving simulator with a very small 2 DOF (roll and pitch) motion base. They carried out a study to validate this device in urban underground expressway scenarios. During their study, Hirata et al. (2007) compared the measurements for speed, deceleration and distance behaviour. The results show that the participants tend to drive faster in the simulator environment for both speed levels (60 and 100 km/h), but there were no significant differences between both test conditions (chosen vs. required speed). For the acceleration behaviour, the authors compare the real-world condition with the simulator condition, split in a variant with activated motion base and one with deactivated motion base. The results show that the deceleration intensities are significantly higher in both simulator conditions. Additionally, this parameter shows significantly higher values for the static simulator compared to the dynamic version. For the distance behaviour, Hirata et al. (2007) reveal that the participants have chosen a smaller distance in the simulator than in the real-world test runs. However, most of these differences were not significant.
Grabe et al. (2010) compared different visualisation systems (screen or HMD without head tracking) in a dynamic driving simulator with regard to the drivers’ performances without comparison to a real car. In the test runs, the participants should complete a slalom course and drive as smoothly as possible through the gates. The results show significant differences in the driving performance between both variants. In the HMD-based variant participants miss 27 gates in comparison with only one gate in the variant with the screen.
In conclusion, the discussed studies show that even if the simulator infrastructures are similar, the scenario and other simulation components could also have an influence on the driver behaviour. Investigators have to critically check, whether their simulators are appropriate for a specific research or not. However, in studies in HMD-based simulators, the proportion without validation is very high. For this, it is important to collect more experience using HMD-based driving simulators.
5 Methodology
In this paper, the results of a validation study are reported. During the investigations, test runs performed in two settings of an HMD-based driving simulator are compared to

the same test runs in a real car. The settings in the simulator only differ in the state of the motion base (disabled vs. enabled). The test runs are divided into 4 different scenarios:
• Straight, • Curves, • Overtaking and • Junction.
The main focus of the validation was on gaze behaviour. This parameter is an important indicator to evaluate the driver behaviour, during an interaction with a new car interior concept in early development phases. Five different areas of interest (AOI) were defined to measure the attention ratio in different areas of the cockpit:
• Road scene ahead, • Inside mirror, • Instrument cluster (dashboard), • Side window driver side (contains outside mirror driver
side), • Side window passenger side (contains outside mirror
passenger side).
Percentage AOI attention ratio (ISO15007-1: 2014) is used as dependent variable. Because the goal for this study is to research the validity of primary driving tasks in these simulator environments, driving dynamics parameters are also measured. In this study, velocity, acceleration, deceleration and steering behaviour are chosen.
5.1 Hypotheses
Since driving simulators are often divided into static and dynamic systems in the literature, the main aim of this study is to validate these both types. Therefore, the following two main hypotheses are defined:
H1: There is no significant difference between the objective validity parameters (driver behaviour—and driving dynamics parameters) in the static HMD-based driving simulator compared to the test runs in a real car (absolute comparison between one specific simulator setup and test runs in a real car).
H2: There is no significant difference between the objective validity parameters (driver behaviour—and driving dynamics parameters) in the dynamic HMD-based driving simulator compared to the test runs in a real car (absolute comparison between one specific simulator setup and test runs in a real car).

