5.2 Driving simulator, test car and measurement systems
For the test runs in the virtual environment the HMD-based driving simulator of the whole-vehicle development department of the Volkswagen AG was used (Fig. 2).
The simulator consists of an electro-mechanical hexapod. The cylinders have motion capacities of 300 mm. A variable seat buck is mounted on the motion base. In this seat buck, 16 linear motors enable the investigators to adjust the primary contact elements (steering wheel, pedals and seat) in the correct ergonomic positions for each car model. Due to the limited capacities of the motion base, an active seat belt loader is mounted on the seat with the goal to improve the feeling of deceleration during braking manoeuvres. This apparatus simulates braking forces relating to the brake pedal position. Head-phones with noise cancelling are used for the sound simulation consisting of engine-, wind-, tiresand traffic sound. For visualisation an HMD is used. During the test runs an HTC Vive Pro Eye with integrated eye tracking was used (State Autumn 2019). A detailed overview of the technical components of the simulator was published in the ATZ worldwide 2019 (Hartfiel et al. 2019).
To minimise bias due to different test environments the variable seat buck was adjusted to a Golf VII model in preparation and the vehicle dynamics model was customised to the parameters of the used test car (2.0 TDI with automatic transmission). Additionally, a diesel sound sample and the comparable cockpit visualisation was integrated in the simulator. For the test runs in the real world, a Golf VII with 2.0 TDI and automatic transmission was used (Fig. 3).
For the measurements in the real car, investigators used glasses from Ergoneers (Dikablis professional) for the eye tracking parameters. Additionally, a TTX-DataLogger from TTTech Group was connected with all Can-buses of the car to measure the driving dynamics parameters. In the

