simulator, a measurement tool developed from the company Cruden was used. This tool measured the data at 10 Hz.
5.3 Proving ground/scenario
The test runs in the real world were performed on a training area for novice drivers near Brunswick. There were some basic rules on the test ground. The participants had to maintain a top speed of 30 km/h. One other test vehicle (traffic car) on the test ground was required for the test runs with interaction between two traffic participants. The whole area consisted of different curve combinations, junctions and also straight roads with different traffic sign combinations.
The virtual scene was modelled as closely as possible to the real one. To this end, georeferenced data from the whole test ground were imported in a scenario editor tool. Based on these correct data, all streets were rebuilt with the original dimensions and all traffic signs were oriented in the correct positions. Additionally, native tree, bushes and other plants were positioned in a way that the whole scene looked very similar to its real counterpart (Fig. 4).
6 Study design
The study was divided into three parts, two simulator slots (static and dynamic) and one slot in the real car. Each participant drove once in each test environment (within subjects). The order was randomized to reduce influences of training effects in the results. Each slot was carried out on a different day to avoid influences of simulator sickness. The order of the different scenarios was not randomised. This was decided to reduce the proportion of incomplete data sets. Before the test runs started, a calibration of the eye tracking systems was carried out. Each slot included a 5 min familiarisation phase at the beginning. During this phase, each participant drove the same route to avoid initial influences

