At first all samples are tested for normal distribution. If there was normal distribution, one-factor repeated-measures analysis of variance (ANOVA) was carried out. For this, Mauchly tests for sphericity were performed. This test checked the homogeneity of the correlations between the different factor levels. If there was a significant result, then the correlations of the samples would not be homogeneous. In these cases, Greenhouse–Geisser-corrections were conducted. If there was sphericity, test for innersubject effects could be carried out without corrections. During the tests of the innersubject effects, repeated-measures ANOVAs were calculated. If the repeated-measures factors are p ≤ 0.05, the influence of the parameter is significant. In these cases, post hoc analysis between different levels (static simulator, dynamic simulator and real car) of the factor were performed. For this purpose, pairwise comparisons of mean values (t test for dependent variables) with Bonferroni correction were carried out in order to investigate which mean values differ significantly. If the repeated-measures ANOVA shows no significant difference between the three conditions, it was assumed that there is absolute validity for these values in the simulator environments.
If the test for normal distribution was negative, repeated-measures ANOVA was not possible. In these cases, Friedman’s ANOVA as a nonparametric test was carried out. If the Friedman tests were significant (p ≤ 0.05), this was a sign that there is an influence of the repeated-measures factor. In the next step, a post-hoc analysis was also performed. Since there were no normal distributions, the Wilcoxon rank-sum test was used. For these tests, Bonferroni corrections were also considered. Because of these corrections, the initial level of significance (α = 0.05) value must be divided by the number of measurement repeats (k = 3). This led to a level of significance of α = 0.0167. P-values where this adjusted level of significance was used, are marked by an “a”.

