8 Discussion
8.1 Gaze behaviour
For the entire study, results show no significant differences for both simulator conditions compared to the real-world test runs. That means that these two HMD-based simulator variants generate absolute valid gaze behaviour with regard to the parameter “percentage attention ratio”. Therefore, both hypotheses can be maintained. A closer look at the different mean values for the entire study show that participants looked less often at the road ahead and more often at the instrument cluster in the static simulator, compared to the dynamic simulator and the real-world test runs. Values suggest that in the static simulator for the participants it could have been more difficult to estimate the current velocity. However, findings could additionally mean that participants do not have the risk awareness that they would have in the real car. The values for the dynamic simulator are in both cases closer to real conditions. This allows the claim that speed feeling and also risk awareness are more realistic in the dynamic simulator condition.
Results of the scenario “Straight” support the general findings. In this scenario, the difference for the parameter “percentage attention ratio” becomes significant between the static simulator and the real-world condition for the AOIs “road scene ahead” and “instrument cluster”. In addition, for both simulator conditions, values for the AOI

“eyes closed or untracked area” show significant differences in comparison with the real test runs. A possible reason for these differences could be the use of different measurement systems or the not completely identical AOIs. Although the AOIs are the same in the simulator conditions, in the real car they have to be redefined. For the static simulator, this means that the hypothesis must be rejected because of the significant differences for 3 AOIs. But the differences are small enough for relative validity to be assumed. The values for the AOI “Eyes closed or untracked area” are not a directly measured parameter. Therefore, it can be summarised that the dynamic simulator condition generates absolute validity for this scenario because no other AOI shows significant differences.
In the scenario “Curves”, for all AOIs except the AOI “side window passenger side”, the values show absolute validity for both simulator conditions. For the AOI “side window passenger side” the values reveal significant differences between the two simulator conditions compared to the real test runs. The reason could be that in the real car, when taking sharp curves, participants moved their eyes more than in the simulator conditions. Therefore, sometimes the eye tracking system could not track the pupils. This thesis is supported by the results for the AOI “Eyes closed or untracked area”, which contains these durations. In this scenario, the values are higher in the real car compared to the simulator conditions. In addition, the reduced horizontal FOV of the HMD (110°) could cause the participants to have to turn their heads more in the simulator to look at the curves. As a result, it’s possible that they look more often through the side window. Therefore, both hypotheses cannot be maintained for this scenario. But because only one AOI show small differences between the two simulator conditions and the real car, it can be assumed that both simulator conditions show relative validity.
In the scenario “Overtaking”, all AOIs except the AOI “Inside mirror” show absolute validity. The values for this AOI show that participants looked significantly more often in the inside mirror in the static simulator condition compared to the real test runs. A reason for these differences could again be the reduced FOV and also the low resolution of the HMD, which could have led to the participants having difficulty seeing the other vehicle in the mirror. In addition, the mirrors in the simulator conditions support no movement parallax during the study. Based on the findings, it can be concluded that the static simulator generates only relative validity in this scenario. However, the results for the dynamic simulator show absolute validity.
In the scenario “Junction”, for all AOIs except the AOI “Inside mirror” results show no significant difference. For the AOI “Inside mirror”, only the static simulator condition shows significant differences. Therefore, it can be argued

