that the static simulator generates only relative validity. However, in the dynamic simulator participants show absolute valid gaze behaviour with regard to the parameter “percentage attention ratio”.
8.2 Driving dynamics parameters
For the analysis for the entire study, the results reveal that values for the parameters “mean max. acceleration” and “mean max. deceleration” are significantly different in both simulator conditions in comparison with the real-world test runs. These findings are also confirmed in the four scenarios. On the one hand, the reason could be that in both simulator conditions the motion cues are not intensive enough that the participants feel the acceleration. But the results for the parameters show no systematic differences. In the scenarios “Straight” and “Curves”, the dynamic simulator shows higher mean max. values, whereas in the scenarios “Overtaking” and “Junction” the static simulator generates higher values. Therefore, it can be also argued that the car model in the simulator is not good enough at the moment. The results for the steering parameters (mean max. steering angle and number of changes of directions) also reveal significant differences for the general overview (all scenarios). Because the differences were comparably big, validity cannot be assumed for both simulators for these four parameters. For the parameters “mean max. velocity” and “mean velocity”, the results show in general (all scenarios) no significant differences and therefore it can be assumed that both simulator conditions show absolute validity with regard to these mean values.
In the scenario “Straight”, the results reveal that the values for the parameter “mean max. velocity” show no significant differences between the simulator conditions and the real-world test runs. For the parameter “mean velocity”, the results show that only the dynamic condition shows absolute validity, whereas in the static simulator participants drove significantly faster. These findings support the results from the gaze behaviour, where participants also had difficulties maintaining the correct speed and looked more often to the instrument cluster. The results for the parameter “mean max. steering angle” reveal no significant differences, whereas the parameter “number of changes of directions” shows significant differences for both simulator conditions. Maybe for participants it is more difficult to stabilize the car in the simulator conditions than in the real car. Reason can be again the difference between the virtual car model compared to the real car.
In the scenario “Curves”, only the parameters “mean max. velocity” and “mean velocity” show no significant differences for the simulator conditions. Therefore, absolute validity can be also assumed only for these two parameter for both simulator conditions. The differences for the

acceleration and deceleration values are too big to assume relative validity. However, for the parameters “mean max. steering angle” and “number of changes of directions” the differences are limited. Therefore, for these parameters relative validity can be assumed.
In the scenario “Overtaking”, the results show that the static simulator generates only for the parameter “mean max. velocity” and “mean max. steering angle” absolute validity, whereas for all other parameters significant differences occurred. For the parameters “mean velocity” and “number of changes of directions”, these differences are small enough to assume relative validity. But for the parameters “mean max. acceleration” and “mean max. deceleration”, the differences are too big, which is why no validity can be assumed. The values for the dynamic simulator reveal absolute validity for the parameters “mean max. velocity”, “mean velocity” and “mean max. steering angle”. In particular, the results for the velocity parameters support the findings that with the additional motion cues it is easier for participants to hold the correct velocity. The results for the parameters “mean max. acceleration”, “mean max. deceleration” show big differences as to why validity cannot be assumed. For the parameter “number of changes of directions”, the differences between the simulator conditions and the real test runs in the real car are small enough that relative validity can be assumed.
In the scenario “Junction” only for the parameter “number of changes of directions”, the results show absolute validity for both simulator variants. For the parameter “mean max. velocity”, only the dynamic simulator shows absolute validity, whereas the static condition generates relative validity of driver behaviour. For the parameter “mean velocity”, the results reveal that the both simulator conditions generate relative validity. The values show that the dynamic condition is closer to the real-world condition. These findings also support the general findings of this study. For the parameters “mean max. acceleration”, “mean max. deceleration” and “mean max. steering angle”, the results show no validity. This also supports the findings that probably the parameters of the car model were not close enough to the real car.
9 Conclusion
In this study, the main goal was to evaluate the capabilities to generate realistic driver behaviour in two different HMDbased driving simulators compared to tests runs in a real car. The use case behind these investigations is the subjective evaluation of new car interior concepts during driving situations in early development phases. The results show that in general, for the chosen gaze behaviour parameter, both simulator conditions generate at least relative validity for all AOIs in all scenarios. However, it is important to note

