that in most cases the values for the dynamic simulator are closer to the real-world conditions. These findings are also confirmed by the statistical analysis. It becomes obvious that in the static simulator the values for 6 of 30 AOIs reveal significant differences and in the dynamic simulator only 2 of 30 show significantly different mean values. Additionally, the differences in the static simulator occur in central AOIs like “road scene ahead” and “instrument cluster”. For this, especially for the very subjective evaluations of car interior concepts, it is recommended to expand a virtual environment with motion cues as soon as evaluations are necessary during driving.
In addition, driving dynamics parameters (velocity, acceleration, deceleration, steering behaviour) are analysed in this study. For the two velocity parameters, the results show that in 4 of 10 measured cases the values for the static simulator show significant differences, whereas in the dynamic simulator only 1 of 10 cases show such different values. Together with the findings for the gaze behaviour, these results suggest that participants had difficulties holding a given velocity in the static condition, additionally showing less risk awareness. Both results are indicators that participants accepted the dynamic simulation more as driving in a car than a static simulation. In addition, the results reveal the importance of correct car models for the validity of driver behaviour. Most of the driving dynamics values for the parameters acceleration, deceleration, and steering behaviour reveal no validity (17 of 30 parameters in both simulator variants). Additionally, for these parameters the results suggest that the motion cues can have a positive influence with regard to more realistic driving behaviour.
For this study, only rough AOIs were chosen because the primary goal was the basic validation of driving in HMDbased simulators. Next steps should be comparable evaluations for more detailed and concrete use cases such as interaction with touch screens to find out more about the possibilities of HMD-based driving simulators.
